'' THE CURRENT DIRECTORY IS SEARCHED FOR LIBRARIES, LAST. fbc does -- measured, its
'' link line ends '-L "<its own lib>" -L "."' -- and this did not, which was a
'' deliberate omission when the library search was written and a wrong one.
''
'' The cost was not theoretical, and it was found by testing the SHIPPED toolchain
'' rather than the dev build: 'tiko -dll thing.bas' writes thing.dll and
'' libthing.dll.a into the working directory, and 'tiko -l thing use.bas' then could
'' not find either, because the only paths searched were the bundled ones under the
'' compiler. Building a library and linking against it beside itself is the obvious
'' workflow.
''
'' LAST, AFTER THE BUNDLED TREE. A stray libfb.a in the working directory must not
'' win against the runtime shipped with the compiler.
''
'' This builds a static library into tests/driver and then links against it by NAME
'' with no -p at all, so the only way it resolves is the cwd entry. TKO_LIBPATH names
'' the runtime because it REPLACES the bundled search -- and the '.' entry is added
'' even then, which is the other half of what this pins.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -target windows-x86_64 -lib tests/codegen/src/export.bas -o tests/driver/libcwdprobe.a
'' ENV: TKO_LIBPATH=build/obj/rtlib/windows-x86_64
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-FILE: tests/driver/libcwdprobe.a
